# =============================================================================
# .eslintignore — ESLint Exclusion List for Trinket OSS Security SAST
# =============================================================================
# SECURITY: This file scopes the ESLint security plugin to the auditable code
# surface only. It uses gitignore-style syntax per ESLint convention.
#
# SECURITY: ESLint security plugin targets (per AAP §0.5.2 Strategy I / R9):
#   lib/, config/, serverside/python/manager/, serverside/r/manager/,
#   serverside/java/manager/, serverside/pygame/manager/
#
# SECURITY: Verification command (per AAP §0.10.1):
#   npx eslint lib/ config/ serverside/python/manager/ serverside/r/manager/ \
#     serverside/java/manager/ serverside/pygame/manager/ --ext .js --no-fix
#
# SECURITY: Maintenance discipline — when adding a new exclusion, prefix the
# block with a "# SECURITY:" comment that cites the AAP rationale. This file
# is part of the audit trail and must remain greppable for security review.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 1 — Frozen Frontend Exclusions (AAP Frontend Freeze Directive / ADR-5)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: AngularJS 1.3.20 SPA is FROZEN per ADR-5 and AAP §0.1.2 Frontend
# Freeze Directive: "DO NOT alter the AngularJS 1.3.20 frontend ... note CVEs,
# assess exploitability in sandboxed context, document findings only."
# SECURITY: Per AAP §0.4.1 Vendored frontend assets — jQuery 2.2.4, AngularJS
# 1.3.20, Ace Editor 1.2.6.1rc2, Skulpt fork, Blockly fork, GlowScript fork,
# Foundation 5.5.3.1, jq-console, CryptoJS AES are all frozen.
# SECURITY: Excluding public/ from SAST prevents accidental modification and
# eliminates noise from frozen-by-policy code. CVEs in these assets are
# tracked in the residual risk register (Deliverable #7), not via ESLint.
# SECURITY: See AAP §0.9.2 Out of Scope — "AngularJS 1.3.20 SPA migration",
# "iframe sandbox attribute changes", and §0.7.3 — "public/js/** and
# public/components/** are frozen — exclude from any tooling".
public/

# SECURITY: Vendored Skulpt fork (browser-side Python interpreter) — frozen
# per AAP Frontend Freeze; defense-in-depth excludes the path explicitly even
# though parent public/ already covers it (intent documentation).
public/js/skulpt/

# SECURITY: Vendored embed runtime (Skulpt, Blockly, GlowScript forks) hosting
# the iframe-sandboxed execution surface — frozen per AAP §0.4.1 and protected
# by the iframe sandbox attribute (no allow-same-origin) per AAP §0.9.2.
public/js/embed/

# SECURITY: Bower-installed third-party components (jQuery 2.2.4, Foundation
# 5.5.3.1, Ace Editor, AngularJS modules) — frozen per ADR-5; already in
# .gitignore at "public/components" and not committed to the repo, but listed
# here for environments where the vendored bundle has been extracted from the
# public-components.tgz release artifact.
public/components/

# SECURITY: Vendored third-party libraries — historically used Bower vendor
# directory; same freeze rationale as public/components/.
public/vendor/

# SECURITY: Frontend partials (AngularJS templates) consumed by the frozen
# SPA — modification is prohibited per AAP Frontend Freeze Directive.
public/partials/

# SECURITY: Compiled frontend CSS — Vite/Sass build output (per
# vite.config.mjs); not JavaScript SAST target.
public/css/

# SECURITY: Static font assets — not JavaScript SAST target.
public/fonts/

# SECURITY: Static image assets — not JavaScript SAST target.
public/img/

# SECURITY: Runtime "published" trinket cache directory — gitignored per
# .gitignore at "public/_published"; user-generated content, not source code.
public/_published/

# SECURITY: Compiled frontend JavaScript bundle output — not source code.
public/js/dist/

# SECURITY: SCSS source for Foundation 5 + Trinket theme — Sass language,
# not a JavaScript SAST target. Compiled by Vite via vite.config.mjs.
static/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 2 — Standard Build Artifacts and Dependencies
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: Third-party dependencies are scanned by `npm audit` (per AAP
# §0.10.1) — not by ESLint. SAST on node_modules/ would produce overwhelming
# noise from package-internal patterns and is universally excluded from
# ESLint by convention.
node_modules/

# SECURITY: Generated lockfiles are not source code — version control discipline
# preserves them but they are not authored. Same rationale for the rollback
# baseline snapshot mandated by AAP Risk Management (§0.5.4): "cp
# package-lock.json package-lock.baseline.json".
package-lock.json
package-lock.baseline.json
serverside/python/manager/package-lock.json
serverside/r/manager/package-lock.json
serverside/java/manager/package-lock.json
serverside/pygame/manager/package-lock.json

# Build outputs and caches — not authored source code
.cache/
dist/
build/
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# Vite/Sass compilation artifacts (covered above for public/, but also at root
# for completeness in case operators relocate output)
.sass-cache/

# Release artifacts produced by the upstream public-components tarball
public-components.tgz

# Minified or bundled JavaScript — third-party output, not source
*.min.js
*.bundle.js

# Map files (sourcemaps) — generated artifacts
*.js.map


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 3 — Test Fixtures and Generated Test Data
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: Test fixtures are intentionally crafted artifacts (e.g., malformed
# inputs, malicious payloads for security regression tests in test/security/);
# linting them generates false-positive findings on patterns that are
# deliberately included. Per AAP §0.6.1 — only test/security/* test modules
# are SAST-scanned; fixture data files are excluded.
test/data/
*.fixture.js
*.fixture.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 4 — Tooling Config Files (per AAP §0.6.1 detailed instructions)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: ESLint configuration itself — meta-config that uses lint-flagging
# patterns (e.g., property names matching detect-object-injection rules) which
# would create circular noise. The configuration file's security posture is
# reviewed via the // SECURITY: annotations within it, not via self-linting.
.eslintrc.js

# SECURITY: Vite bundler config (ES module syntax, build-time only) — not part
# of the runtime application code surface. CSS/asset bundling configuration.
vite.config.mjs


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 5 — Security Audit / Report / Tooling Output Artifacts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: Per AAP §0.10.1 verification commands — these are tool outputs
# from the security toolchain (npm audit, Trivy, OWASP ZAP, ESLint cache).
# They are generated artifacts and must not be SAST-scanned.

# OWASP ZAP baseline scan reports (per AAP §0.10.1 zap-baseline-report.html)
zap-baseline-report.*

# Aqua Trivy container scan reports (per AAP §0.10.1 trivy image scans)
trivy-report.*

# npm audit JSON/text reports (per AAP §0.10.1 npm audit --audit-level=high)
npm-audit-report.*

# ESLint own cache file (generated by --cache flag if used in CI)
.eslintcache


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operational Exclusions — Logs, Migrations, Backups, Editor Files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: Mongoose migration backup snapshots (per .gitignore at
# "lib/migrations/backups") — operator runtime artifacts, not source code.
lib/migrations/backups/

# SECURITY: Runtime/development config layers (per .gitignore) that may
# contain operator secrets (AWS keys, SMTP credentials, session passwords).
# Defense-in-depth: confirm no secret-bearing local config is ever sent
# through ESLint output channels, even though the file extensions are .yaml.
config/runtime.json
config/runtime.json.tmp-*
config/local.yaml
config/local.yml
config/local.json
config/development.yaml
config/development.yml
config/development.json
config/production.yaml
config/production.yml
config/production.json
config/migration.json
config/migrationDb.json

# Build/run logs
*.log
npm-debug.log
build.log

# Editor and OS artifacts — not source code
*.swp
*.DS_Store
*.sublime-project
*.sublime-workspace

# Vagrant artifacts (legacy, per .gitignore)
.vagrant/

# SECURITY: Blitzy platform internal artifacts (screenshots, working
# directory). Not part of the application source code surface.
blitzy/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-Inclusions (Negations) — Explicit SAST Targets
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY: ESLint applies ignore patterns top-to-bottom; later negations
# (prefixed with `!`) re-include matched paths. The exclusions above are
# scoped narrowly so negations are not currently required, but the pattern
# is documented here for future maintenance: if a security audit needs to
# pull a specific frozen-frontend file under SAST coverage, add a negation
# below with a "# SECURITY:" rationale comment citing the AAP finding.
# Example (commented):
#   !public/js/trinket-share.js  # SECURITY: targeted audit per AAP-D2
